winebus: let the haptics device be asked to declare no button at all.

An experiment with a registry value, not a fix, and the default does not move.

THE OBSERVATION, from the person playing rather than from a trace: Ghost of
Tsushima does not keep L3 held -- sprint will not stay down -- while
XInputRumble is on. With it off, it does. The pad is recognised as a Sony
controller either way, with PlayStation glyphs, so this is not the pad falling
back to something else.

THE CANDIDATE, in our own code. mgvf-0010's haptics device declares ONE BUTTON
THAT IS NEVER PRESSED. It is there so that a client counting button caps does
not see zero. A game that polls the pad and xinput and merges or last-writes the
two would see a device reporting every button released on every poll, which is
the exact shape of a held button being cleared.

That is a candidate and not a diagnosis. The game may equally be doing something
this driver has nothing to do with, and nothing here has measured it. What this
patch provides is the one-bit experiment that tells the two apart:
XInputRumbleStubButton 0 builds the same device with the button gone and a byte
of padding in its place, so the input report stays one byte and every offset
downstream is unmoved.

WHY IT IS NOT THE DEFAULT, and this is the whole reason it is an option rather
than a deletion. The reason the button exists has not stopped being true.
xinput's try_add_device allocates from NumberInputButtonCaps BEFORE it checks
the count:

    if (!(button_caps = malloc(sizeof(*button_caps) * ...NumberInputButtonCaps))) return FALSE;

With no button caps at all that is a malloc(0), and a malloc(0) that returns
NULL makes xinput refuse the device outright. The failure mode of guessing wrong
here is no rumble at all rather than a rumble with one fault, which is worse
than the thing being tested. So the safer packet stays the default and the
experiment is the thing that has to be asked for.

HOW TO READ THE RESULT. The trace says which descriptor went out. If L3 holds
with the button gone, the candidate is the cause and the next question is how to
keep xinput happy without it. If L3 still does not hold, the button was never
the problem and this option should be forgotten rather than kept -- an option
that tested a wrong idea is clutter, and this file will say so.

A NOTE ON HOW THIS PATCH WAS MADE, because it cost half an hour and would cost
it again. The first version of this file was diffed against a baseline that had
been rebuilt by applying an earlier patch with fuzz (-F3). Fuzz let that apply
"succeed" at a shifted offset, so the baseline was subtly wrong and the diff
inherited it: 262 lines where the real change is 121, and mgvf-0020 then refused
to reverse off the result, which made build-controller-bus.sh report six patches
as unapplied and try to apply one of them again.

A baseline for a new patch is reconstructed by exact string surgery on the tree,
never by re-applying the stack with fuzz, and the check that it is right is that
the WHOLE existing chain reverses off it cleanly. Then the new patch is verified
the other way: reversing it off the tree has to reproduce that baseline byte for
byte.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
