+    /* MGVF mgvf-0029: the same device with the button taken out, and one byte
+     * of padding in its place so the input report is still one byte long and
+     * every offset downstream is unmoved.
+     *
+     * WHAT IT IS FOR. The button above is never pressed -- it exists so that a
+     * client counting button caps does not see zero. A game that polls both the
+     * pad and xinput and merges the two would see a device reporting every
+     * button released on every poll, which is the shape of a held button being
+     * cleared, and Ghost of Tsushima does not keep L3 held while XInputRumble
+     * is on. That is an observation with a candidate, not a diagnosis, and this
+     * is how the candidate gets tested: one registry value, no rebuild.
+     *
+     * WHY IT IS NOT THE DEFAULT. The reason the button was put there still
+     * stands. xinput's try_add_device allocates from NumberInputButtonCaps
+     * BEFORE it checks the count: with no button caps at all that is a
+     * malloc(0), and a malloc(0) that returns NULL makes it refuse the device
